            <ArticleTitle>Provocation tests versus 24-h pH and pressure measurements.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1141-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The diagnosis of the oesophageal origin of non-cardiac chest pain is still a matter of controversy. This review discusses the diagnostic accuracy of oesophageal provocation tests, such as acid perfusion, edrophonium and balloon distension, in comparison with 24-h intra-oesophageal pH and pressure recordings. It also discusses how these various tests relate to known underlying mechanisms of oesophageal chest pain: acid reflux, oesophageal dysmotility or a combination of both.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
